1) - edit the parameter file (example parameterscan_settings.input)
     and the Mathematica file (example parameterscan.m) as usual;
   - in the parameter file specify some values for the symbols 
     defining your numerical constants;
   - set "exeflag=1" in the parameter file
   - changing the name of the point according to the value for the 
     numerical constants will be done automatically
   
2) edit multiparam.input to specify the name of your parameter file,
   and the prefix you would like to use for the names of the points
   to be calculated, eg
   paramfile=parameterscan_settings.input
   pointname=mpp
   note: parameterscan_settings.input, parameterscan.m, parameterscan_scan.input
   should be in the same directory, can be any of your working directories.
   
   next define sets of parameter values you wish to calculate:
   
   If you wish to specify particular numerical points this can also be
   done by listing them in the parameterscan_scan.input file via the syntax:
   0.3,0.2,1
   0.6,0.9,0.5
   etc.
   If you don't wish to calculated all points specified in parameterscan_scan.input,
   and instead only want the first N points, you can specifie this with the
   statement:
   lines=N
   in the parameterscan_scan.input file.

   One can also define min values, maxvalues, steps for each parameter

   The syntax for this is:                                  
   minvals=                                                                        
   maxvals=                                                                        
   stepvals= 
   
   (lists of values matching the list of symbols defined in paramfile)                                                                      

   If you wish to specify a set of values for each parameter which are not
   equally spaced, this is done by using the syntax:
   paramfile=
   values1=v11,v12,...
   values2=v21,v22,...
   ...
   valuesk=vk1,vk2,...   

   
   These different types of syntax can be used alongside each other, for
   an example see demos/parameterscan_scan.input

3) run secdec
  'secdec -g -d workingdir -m parameterscan_scan.m -p parameterscan_settings.input -k parameterscan_scan.input ' 
  This command will run:
  './launch -d workingdir -m parameterscan_scan.m -p parameterscan_settings.input ' and
  './multinumerics.pl  -d workingdir -p parameterscan_scan.input ' 

                   
================================================================                                                                                
when running on a batch system:                                                        
                                                                                
the syntax is as before: 
to start the algebraic part type 
'secdec -g -d workingdir -m parameterscan_scan.m -p parameterscan_settings.input --algebraic' 

set clusterflag=1 in the parameterfile file and run
'secdec -g -d workingdir -p parameterscan_settings.input -k parameterscan_scan.input --numerics' 
to start the numeric part

when all jobs finished, run 
'secdec -g -d workingdir -p parameterscan_settings.input -k parameterscan_scan.input --collectresults' 
to collect the results
